# KQL's `innerunique`: DS0124 explains the divergence (D-004). The fix changes meaning, so it is not machine-applicable.
mode: syntax
---- query
from 'orders.csv' | join kind = innerunique 'customers.csv' on customerId
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..74
  PIPELINE@0..73
    IDENT@0..4 "from"
    WHITESPACE@4..5 " "
    LITERAL@5..17
      STRING@5..17 "'orders.csv'"
    WHITESPACE@17..18 " "
    PIPE@18..19 "|"
    WHITESPACE@19..20 " "
    JOIN_OP@20..73
      IDENT@20..24 "join"
      WHITESPACE@24..25 " "
      JOIN_KIND@25..43
        IDENT@25..29 "kind"
        WHITESPACE@29..30 " "
        EQ@30..31 "="
        WHITESPACE@31..32 " "
        IDENT@32..43 "innerunique"
      WHITESPACE@43..44 " "
      LITERAL@44..59
        STRING@44..59 "'customers.csv'"
      WHITESPACE@59..60 " "
      IDENT@60..62 "on"
      WHITESPACE@62..63 " "
      NAME_REF@63..73
        IDENT@63..73 "customerId"
  WHITESPACE@73..74 "\n"

error[DS0124]: `innerunique` is not supported
  --> query:1:33
  |
1 | from 'orders.csv' | join kind = innerunique 'customers.csv' on customerId
  |                                 ^^^^^^^^^^^ this is KQL's default join kind
  |
  = help: use `kind = inner`; to keep one left row per key, as KQL does, remove the duplicates first, for example with `distinct`
  = note: unlike KQL, DataScript never drops duplicate keys from the left side; the default join kind is `inner`
